<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-reals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Real Numbers</title>

    <question>
      <p>What is a "number"?</p>
    </question>



    <observation>
      <p>Things we think of as "numbers" seem to have the following properties:</p>
          <aside>
            <p>In particular, these operations should both be commutative and associative, and obey distributive rules.</p>
          </aside>
        <ol>
          <li>There are operations <m>+</m> and <m>\times</m> that behave as we expect.</li>
          <li>There are identity elements: <m>0</m> for addition and <m>1</m> for multiplication.</li>
          <li>These operations have inverse operations <m>-</m> and <m>\div</m>, respectively.</li>
          <li>Every number has an additive inverse and every non-zero number has a multiplicative inverse.</li>
        </ol>
      <p>Mathematicians call anything with these properties a <term>field</term>.</p>
    </observation>

    <example>
      <p>The set of rational numbers, denoted <m>\Q</m>, is a field.</p>
    </example>

    <example>
      <p>The set of <term>constructible numbers</term> are the lengths that can be
      constructed with a compass and straight edge, given a line segment of length <m>1</m>.
      The number <m>\sqrt{2}</m> is constructible, but <m>\sqrt[3]{2}</m> is not.
      </p>
      <aside>
        <p>A classical geometry question asks if, given a cube, another cube of twice
        the volume can be constructed with a compass and a straight edge.  Showing
        that this is impossible is equivalent to showing that <m>\sqrt[3]{2}</m> is
        not constructible and took over 2000 years to solve.
        </p>
      </aside>
      <p>
      The set of constructible numbers also forms a field.
      </p>
    </example>

    <example>
      <p>The set of rational functions, i.e. functions of the form <m>\dfrac{p(x)}{q(x)}</m>,
        where <m>p(x)</m> and <m>q(x)</m> are polynomials, is a field.
      </p>
    </example>

    <observation>
      <p>The rational numbers (and the constructible numbers) are an <term>ordered field</term>: we have an inequality operator <m>\lt</m> such that
        for any two distinct rational numbers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, either <m>a\lt b</m> or <m>b \lt a</m>.
        </p>
    </observation> 

    <question>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Previously we asked the question: Does <m>0.999999\ldots=1</m>?
        </p>
        <p>
           How can we interpret <m>0.999999\ldots</m> as a number?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </question>

    <definition>
      <statement>
        <p>Informally, <term>sequence</term> (of numbers) is an infinite list of numbers.</p>
        <p>Formally, a rational sequence is a function from the natural numbers to 
        the rational numbers.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example>
      <p>
        <m>0.9,0.99,0.999,0.9999,0.99999,\ldots</m> is a (rational) sequence.
        It represents the function from <m>\N</m> to <m>\Q</m> given by
        <md>n \rightarrow 1-\frac{1}{10^{n+1}}.</md>
      </p>
      <p>Often we use a notation such as <m>\{a_n\} _{n=0} ^\infty</m> for a sequence.
      In this example, we would further write <m>a_n=1-\frac{1}{10^{n+1}}</m>, or perhaps
      even write <m>\{1-\frac{1}{10^{n+1}}\} _{n=0} ^\infty</m>.
      </p>
    </example>

    <activity>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Write the first 5 terms of each of these sequences
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <m>\{\frac{1}{n}\}_{n=1} ^\infty</m>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <m>\{2^n\}_{n=1} ^\infty</m>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <m>\{\frac{n-1}{n}\}_{n=1} ^\infty</m>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <m>\{(-1)^n\}_{n=1} ^\infty</m>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            How would you describe each of these sequences, particularly as <m>n</m> becomes large?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </activity>

  <activity>
    <introduction>
      <p>Let's look at the sequence <m>\{a_n\}_{n=1} ^\infty</m> given by
      <md>a_n=1+\dfrac{1}{2}+\cdots+\dfrac{1}{n}-\log(n)</md>
      </p>
      <aside>
        <p>We use <m>\log</m> in the usual sense that mathematicians do,
        that is to refer to the natural log (base <m>e</m>).
        </p>
      </aside>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <p>
        Use a computational tool to write down the first 10 terms of this sequence.
      </p>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Show that every term of this sequence is positive.</p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Interpret <m>1+\dfrac{1}{2}+\cdots+\dfrac{1}{n}</m> as an area
          or a Riemann sum.
        </p>
      </hint>
  </activity>
    

</section>